{"guide":{"questionsToConsider":["Are you researching a new product, a whole range or just your customers in general? Cut the questions that don't feed that decision.","Do you need the age question, and can you explain why you're asking?","Which ingredients or concerns matter for your products? Add them to the goals list.","If people mention a reaction, who reads those answers and what do they do with them?"],"howToUseResponses":"Compare goals and routine length by skin type to see which groups your range serves well and which it misses. The ranking of choice factors tells you what to lead with on labels and product pages. Read the reaction answers for patterns in product types and ingredients, and invite testers whose skin type and goals match the product you're developing.","customizeSteps":["Edit the product and goal options to reflect your own range and the concerns you focus on.","Put your brand name in the greeting and say what you'll do with the answers.","Share the link after a purchase or with your mailing list, and export tester emails to CSV when you're ready to send samples."],"faqs":[{"q":"What questions should a skin care survey ask?","a":"Skin type, goals, the products someone uses now, how happy they are with their routine, what matters when they choose a product and where they buy."},{"q":"Can a skin care survey diagnose skin problems?","a":"No. It records what people tell you about their habits and preferences. Anyone with a skin concern should see a doctor or dermatologist."},{"q":"How do I use a skin care survey to recruit product testers?","a":"Ask at the end whether they want to test products and collect an email only from those who say yes. You can then pick testers by skin type and goals."},{"q":"Should I ask for age in a skincare survey?","a":"It can help, since routines and concerns change with age, but make it optional and include a prefer not to say choice."}]},"doc":{"schemaVersion":10,"title":"Skin care survey","description":"Learn how 